ValkyOS
=======

ValkyOS is an independent Android ROM based on **LineageOS 24.0 (Android 17)** that
adds privacy and security features ported from **GrapheneOS**. The main feature is
sandboxed Google Play (GmsCompat): Play Store and Play services run as regular,
unprivileged apps.

This repository (`ValkyOS/android`, branch `17`) is the **ValkyOS manifest**. It is
LineageOS's manifest plus [`snippets/valkyos.xml`](snippets/valkyos.xml), which:

- replaces the projects ValkyOS changes with its forks (branch `valkyos-17`);
- adds `vendor/valkyos`, GmsCompat, GmsCompatConfig, the GrapheneOS App Store and
  Vanadium.

The rest of `default.xml` is exactly LineageOS's, so the monthly LineageOS security
patch merges cleanly.

Building ValkyOS is the same as building LineageOS: only the manifest URL changes.
If you have built LineageOS before, you already know the steps.


What you need
-------------

- The Android `repo` tool, Git and Git LFS, and the usual LineageOS build
  requirements: <https://wiki.lineageos.org/>.
- Device sources for your phone that already work on LineageOS 24 (Android 17):
  device tree, kernel and vendor blobs. For a phone supported by LineageOS this is
  automatic; for a phone that is not, you need a local manifest (see below).


Sync
----

```bash
mkdir -p ~/valkyos && cd ~/valkyos
repo init -u https://github.com/ValkyOS/android -b 17 --git-lfs
repo sync -c -j8
```

Do **not** use `LineageOS/android` here: the manifest is this repository.


Add your phone
--------------

**Phone supported by LineageOS.** The usual flow works:

```bash
source build/envsetup.sh
breakfast <device>
```

`breakfast` downloads the device tree, kernel and vendor blobs and creates the local
manifest.

**Phone not supported by LineageOS** (or with a custom device tree, like the
Motorola Edge 50 Pro): put a normal `repo` local manifest in
`.repo/local_manifests/`. It must contain the device tree, kernel, vendor blobs and
any device-only extras.

The Edge 50 Pro example is [`dvy-jp/local_manifests`](https://github.com/dvy-jp/local_manifests)
(branch `valkyos-17`, file `eqe.xml`):

```bash
git clone https://github.com/dvy-jp/local_manifests -b valkyos-17 .repo/local_manifests
repo sync -c -j8
```

Use it as a model for your own phone: the file is a plain `repo` local manifest, with
the exact revisions of the device tree, kernel and vendor sources.


Make your device inherit ValkyOS
--------------------------------

In your `lineage_<device>.mk`, inherit the ValkyOS common configuration:

```make
$(call inherit-product, vendor/valkyos/config/common.mk)
```

That adds:

- the ValkyOS name and version (`ro.valkyos.*`), with the LineageOS build machinery;
- ValkyOS overlays (including the WebView provider and other system defaults);
- the ValkyOS wallpapers;
- GmsCompat (sandboxed Google Play), the GrapheneOS App Store and Vanadium;
- the ValkyOS recovery logo and boot animation.

A device tree may still set its own boot animation or screen width; `TARGET_BOOTANIMATION`
is only a default.


Signing keys
------------

Generate your **own** signing keys following the LineageOS signing procedure and keep
them in `vendor/lineage-priv/keys` (or the directory configured for your build).
Never use someone else's keys: the ROM would be signed by a stranger, and OTA and
verified boot trust would be wrong.

Keys are private: never commit them, never upload them, never share them.


Build
-----

```bash
source build/envsetup.sh
brunch <device>
```

The update package is `out/target/product/<device>/ValkyOS-<version>.zip` (no
`lineage-` prefix).


What ValkyOS adds
-----------------

- **Sandboxed Google Play (GmsCompat)**, ported from GrapheneOS: Play Store and Play
  services are regular, unprivileged apps. Sign-in, app installs and updates, push
  notifications, Google sign-in inside apps and location rerouted to the OS work.
- **Contact Scopes, Storage Scopes and the Sensors permission**, from GrapheneOS.
- **Vanadium** (GrapheneOS browser and WebView) and the **GrapheneOS App Store**.
- **Per-user options**, **GrapheneOS connectivity checks** and the **Play Integrity API
  notification and per-app blocking** (nothing is spoofed).
- **ValkyOS branding**: name in Settings and About phone, logo, seven wallpapers, no
  LineageOS statistics, an Updater with the ValkyOS name and version, and no LineageOS
  legal or contributors entries in About phone.


Privacy
-------

ValkyOS itself collects no data and runs no servers of its own.

- The LineageOS anonymous statistics are disabled and hidden; nothing is sent to
  stats.lineageos.org.
- System updates have no update server yet, so the Updater makes no requests.
- Internet connectivity checks use GrapheneOS servers by default (Settings > Network &
  internet lets you choose GrapheneOS, standard Google servers, or off).
- The GrapheneOS App Store and Vanadium check GrapheneOS servers for updates.
- Network time still uses the standard Android server (time.android.com).
- Google Play is optional and sandboxed; if you install it, Google's own policies apply
  to it.

Limitations
-----------

- The bootloader must stay unlocked (for example, Motorola does not support a custom
  root of trust), so verified boot reports **orange**.
- **Play Integrity is not promised**; apps that require it may refuse to run.
- Sandboxed Google Play must be installed and updated through the GrapheneOS App Store
  (or `adb`), not by arbitrary installers.
- Postponed in GmsCompat: Google Camera client overrides and Android Auto.


Credits
-------

- [LineageOS](https://lineageos.org/): the base.
- [GrapheneOS](https://grapheneos.org/): GmsCompat and the privacy/security features.

ValkyOS is an independent project and is **not** affiliated with or endorsed by
LineageOS or GrapheneOS.
